+"""The user's setup profile and the setup check (phase 4 item 1, stage 2; the user, 2026-10-21).
+
+With LDR_DEEP_SETUP_PROFILE=<user-setup.md> the lead agent's system prompt and the report writer's outline, section and
+opening prompts carry the facts of the user's own computer (as stage 1's "with" replays did: tools/level-replay.py
+setup_block / add_setup_to_report_prompts), and after the citation check a setup check reads the report against those
+facts. It is the safety net for the lead agent, which still ignores the profile sometimes (o02 E1): each statement
+that contradicts the facts gets the fix right after it, marked as the setup check's, and a short "Setup check" section
+lists them. Nothing is deleted or rewritten. Without the variable nothing here runs.
+
+Fix 5 (LDR_DEEP_FIX=baseline, 10-27): the same check also judges every recommendation against the user's current
+settings (the task's or the setup facts': a value already set is not a gain - "already-set") and every gain against
+the user's measured numbers, like with like ("baseline": prompt speed with prompt speed, decode with decode, the same
+context and model). With Fix 2 (inline) or any fix on, quotes are found by checks.Locator (bold, code marks, citations
+and capitals no longer make a quote "not found word for word").
+10-28 (the user): the "baseline" notes are dropped - on p4s6 o02 about 8 of 19 corrections only asked to compare with
+the user's own numbers. The check no longer asks for them, and a "baseline" verdict is not marked; "already-set" stays.
+The writer's own like-with-like rule (report.py, Fix 5) is unchanged.
+"""
+from __future__ import annotations
+
+import json
+import os
+import re
+from pathlib import Path
+
+SETUP_INTRO = ("The user's own computer and setup, as the user gave them (facts, not search results). Where the task "
+               "concerns the user's machine, judge what fits, runs or helps on it from these facts. Do not contradict "
+               "them, do not restate them differently, and do not assume another model or size of a component.")
+REPORT_NOTE = ("Statements about the user's own machine must agree with <user_setup>; where the evidence was gathered "
+               "for another machine (another card, slot or memory size), say so instead of carrying it over.")
+
+SETUPCHECK_PROMPT = """You are the claim-verification step of a research system. Below are the facts of the user's \
+own computer, as the user gave them, and a research report written for this user. Find every statement in the report \
+about the user's own machine or setup: its components and which model or size each is, memory sizes, slots and PCIe \
+links, bandwidths, and what fits, runs or helps on it (including limits and budgets the report derives from them). \
+Judge each one against the facts:
+- "contradicts": it conflicts with the facts, or with what follows directly from them (for example, a card in a slot \
+that runs at x4 gets x4 at most, whatever the card supports). Give the corrected statement.
+- "consistent": the facts support it.
+- "unsupported": the facts neither confirm nor contradict it. That is not an error.
+General statements about other machines are not about the user's setup; leave them out.{only}
+
+<user_setup>
+{setup}
+</user_setup>
+
+<report>
+{report}
+</report>
+
+Reply with JSON only: {{"claims": [{{"quote": "<the statement, copied exactly from the report>", "verdict": \
+"contradicts|consistent|unsupported", "why": "<one sentence>", "fix": "<the corrected statement, or empty>"}}]}}"""
+BASELINE_RULES = """
+The task may describe the setup it is about - a version, settings, measured numbers - and that can be another state \
+than the setup facts (an earlier version, an older measurement). Where the task describes it, judge against the \
+task: a statement that agrees with the task's description is consistent even where the setup facts differ, and is \
+never "corrected" to the setup facts; use the setup facts for what the task does not describe. When the task is \
+about another version or state than the setup facts describe, use the setup facts only for the hardware (components, \
+memory, slots, links), not for software settings, versions or measured speeds.
+Also judge every RECOMMENDATION in the report (a setting, flag, build option or change it advises) against the \
+user's current settings - as the task states them (the task's own settings come first) or else as the facts state them:
+- "already-set": the user already runs it (the same value, or the default already does it on this setup), so it is \
+not a gain; say what the user already has in "fix".
+"""
+TASK_BLOCK = """
+<task>
+{task}
+</task>
+"""
